  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to small sounds. A delivery bicycle clicks over a loose grate. Pigeons negotiate the roofline. Somewhere behind an apartment window, a kettle begins its patient argument with silence.\n\nI like this hour because nothing has fully decided what it will become. The bakery lights are on, but the door remains locked. Buses move almost empty, carrying drivers, nurses, cleaners, and the occasional traveler who watches unfamiliar streets slide past. Even the traffic signals seem ceremonial, changing colors for no one.\n\nSoon, certainty arrives. Shutters rise. Phones ring. Coffee spills. People hurry toward meetings they may not need, rehearsing explanations for delays that have not happened yet. The day acquires edges, prices, passwords, appointments, and names.\n\nBut before all that, there is a brief republic of possibility. In it, every alley might lead somewhere important, every lit window suggests a story, and every stranger appears to be setting out on a quiet adventure.\n\nPerhaps this is why dawn rewards walking. Walking asks almost nothing: only that you continue, notice, and allow the world to be ordinary without becoming invisible. A cracked wall can hold five shades of blue. A tree trapped in pavement can still scatter gold leaves. A tired shopkeeper can lift a metal gate as if raising a curtain.\n\nBy eight o'clock, the republic dissolves. Yet its lesson remains: attention is a form of gratitude. We do not need a grand life to feel wonder. We need only pause long enough to see it.",
